{
 "run_on": "2026-11-17",
 "method": "A replay with no language model and no agent in it; the only model is the small embedding model behind the search by meaning. For every bug-labelled report of a public project that was closed by a merged change and passes the filters listed under \"limits\", the memory is cut back to the day the bug was reported (the bug itself, its fix and everything later removed), the bug's own words are used as the question, and each way of searching the memory returns earlier fixed bugs. A search \"hits\" when a source-code file changed by one of the fixes it returned is a file the real fix of this bug changed. Every bug is asked twice: with its report (the title and the start of the text, up to 1,600 characters), and with its title alone, which stands in for a short question.",
 "limits": [
  "The question put to the memory is the bug's own words: its title with the start of its text (up to 1,600 characters, as the report reads today), and separately its title alone. Neither is what a person or an agent in the middle of a failure would type.",
  "A hit means a returned fix changed one of the source-code files the real fix changed; it does not mean the bug would have been fixed. Tests, documentation and configuration files do not count, on either side.",
  "Not every fixed bug is replayed. Only reports that carry the project's bug label and were closed by a merged change are read, and of those the following are left out: bugs whose fix changed more than 12 files, no source-code file, or more than 8 of them, and bugs reported when fewer than 20 earlier bugs had a merged fix. The counts are in the result under \"selection\".",
  "At most 480 of a project's most recent bug reports are read, so a busy project is represented by its last months.",
  "Intervals and p values count each bug as independent. The stricter reading, with whole projects as the unit, is given beside them (interval_95_by_project): the lead over the simple rules and over keyword search holds there; the small differences between the combined search and search by meaning alone do not.",
  "The first row reads deeper than the product does: 80 records from 320 per half. The row \"read as the hosted tool reads it\" replays the search the way the address for coding agents makes it: the question cleaned and cut to 600 characters, 30 records from 120 per half, the first 20 reports.",
  "Figures move a little between runs. The search service does not fix the order of records whose scores are exactly equal, which changes the product's result for a few bugs in three thousand (the count against the run before is in the result under \"repeat_run\"). The word counts behind the keyword half change with the set of projects stored: the first run of 17 November 2026 stored the projects one at a time, and against it the product's result changed for about twenty bugs, up to two points in one small project, with no net change.",
  "History is cut by day, not by hour. How rare a word is, which the keyword half of the search relies on, is counted over every record stored (all of these projects, at any date), not only over what existed in that project on that day; the hosted service also keeps one count for all the projects it holds.",
  "The embedding model is all-MiniLM-L6-v2 run locally, the same model the hosted service uses. It reads about the first 256 word pieces of a text, so most of a long report is seen only by the keyword half.",
  "A small share of the replayed bugs are imperfect records: for some the fix is a change in another repository, and some are open today although a merged change is linked as closing them. Their counts are in the result under \"selection\" (about 3% together).",
  "No language model and no agent is involved: this measures what the memory contains and how well each search finds it, not what an agent does with it."
 ],
